<section class="story-section" id="problem"><p class="eyebrow">01 / The question before</p><h2>Can a machine<br />hold a conversation?</h2><p>In 1950 Alan Turing opened a paper in the journal <em>Mind</em> with a short line: “I propose to consider the question, ‘Can machines think?’” He swapped that hard question for a game. A judge chats by typed messages with a person and a machine. If the judge cannot reliably tell which is which, the machine has done something worth taking seriously.</p><p>Turing’s test was about text alone. No faces, no voices, just replies. Sixteen years later a program showed how easily that kind of test can be passed in spirit, and how little it can prove.</p></section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea"><p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p><h2>Find a keyword.<br />Turn the sentence around.</h2><p>Joseph Weizenbaum wrote ELIZA at MIT and described it in January 1966. It ran on MIT’s time-sharing system: people typed at a terminal and ELIZA typed back. Its best-known script, DOCTOR, played a nondirective psychotherapist, the kind who mostly turns your words into questions.</p><p>There was no understanding inside. ELIZA scanned your sentence for <strong>keywords</strong> such as “mother”, “always” or “computer”, each with a rank. The top keyword chose a <strong>decomposition rule</strong>, a pattern that cut the sentence into parts, and a <strong>reassembly rule</strong> that put some parts back into a reply. On the way it swapped pronouns, “I” for “you” and “my” for “your”. When nothing matched, it used a line that fits almost anything, such as “Please go on.”</p><p>Weizenbaum later wrote that his secretary, who had watched him build the program for months, began talking to it and after a few exchanges asked him to leave the room. People knew it was a program. They confided in it anyway.</p>
<figure class="idea-diagram eliza-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 400 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="eliza-diagram-title eliza-diagram-desc"><title id="eliza-diagram-title">How ELIZA builds a reply</title><desc id="eliza-diagram-desc">The input “My mother worries about me” has the keyword “my”. A pattern splits it into parts, pronouns are swapped, and a template puts the parts back as “Who else in your family worries about you?”</desc><g fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle"><rect x="10" y="10" width="380" height="56" rx="10" fill="var(--paper)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="200" y="45" font-size="20">“My mother worries about me.”</text><text x="200" y="92" font-size="24">↓</text><rect x="10" y="104" width="380" height="56" rx="10" fill="var(--era-counting)"/><text x="200" y="128" font-size="15" fill="var(--on-accent)">swap: my → your, me → you</text><text x="200" y="150" font-size="15" fill="var(--on-accent)">keyword “my” · pattern * your * (family) *</text><text x="200" y="186" font-size="24">↓</text><rect x="10" y="198" width="380" height="56" rx="10" fill="var(--paper)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="200" y="232" font-size="17">“Who else in your family $5?”</text><text x="200" y="280" font-size="24">↓</text><text x="200" y="318" font-size="19">“Who else in your family worries about you?”</text></g></svg><figcaption>One of this page’s rules, written in the style of the 1966 script. “$5” is the fifth part of the pattern.</figcaption></figure></section>
