<section class="story-section" id="mattered"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p><h2>Fluent is not<br />the same as understanding.</h2><p>People read care and insight into ELIZA’s replies, even when they knew how simple it was. This became known as the <strong>ELIZA effect</strong>: we fill in meaning that a system does not have. It worried Weizenbaum enough that he spent much of his later career warning against handing human judgment to machines.</p><p>Today’s chatbots work very differently. They are not hand-written rules: they learn from huge amounts of text to predict the next piece of a reply, and they can see the whole conversation so far. They can do arithmetic, follow a topic and admit what they don’t know, which ELIZA never could. The ELIZA effect has not gone away, though. A fluent reply is still not proof that anyone is home.</p><a class="next-chapter" href={`/chapters/${next.slug}/`}><span>Next · {next.year}</span><strong>{next.title} ↗</strong><span>{next.hook}</span></a></section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper"><summary>Go deeper <span>How the script machinery works</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body"><h3>Keywords, ranks and links</h3><p>While scanning, ELIZA replaces words that have a substitution (<code>I</code> becomes <code>you</code>) and stacks every keyword it meets, highest rank first. Punctuation splits the input: the first clause that contains a keyword is used and the rest is dropped. A keyword can point to another keyword’s rules, the way <code>dreamt</code> uses the rules for <code>dream</code>, and a rule can give up and pass control to the next keyword on the stack.</p><h3>Reassembly in turn</h3><p>Each decomposition rule has a short list of reassembly templates, used in order. Ask the same kind of thing often enough and the list comes round again, which is why ELIZA repeats itself. Because the pronoun swap happens word by word, before any pattern is matched, <code>I feel angry at you</code> comes back as <code>angry at I</code>.</p><h3>Memory</h3><p>When the keyword is <code>my</code>, ELIZA also files away a second reply built from the same sentence. Later, when an input has no keyword at all, it can bring that reply out instead of a stock phrase, which feels uncannily attentive.</p><h3>What this page changes</h3><p>The rules here are new, written in the style of DOCTOR rather than copied from it. The engine expands <code>I’m</code> and <code>you’re</code> before scanning, answers words in other scripts as the 1966 script answered German, and uses stored memories whenever there is no keyword (Weizenbaum used a counter to decide). It also reports which rule fired, which the original never did.</p></div></details>
